.TH GIT-FETCH-SIGN "1" "October 20, 2026" "git-fetch-sign" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-fetch-sign \- fetch a remote topic branch and sign its commits locally
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git fetch-sign
\fB\-\-remote\fR NAME [BRANCH] [\fB\-\-worktree\fR PATH] [OPTIONS]
.br
.B git fetch-sign
\fB\-\-remote\fR NAME \fB\-\-pr\fR NUMBER|URL
[\fB\-\-repo\fR OWNER/REPO]
[\fB\-\-worktree\fR PATH] [OPTIONS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Fetch BRANCH from the configured remote selected by --remote, sign its
unsigned topic commits, verify them, then replace the same
local branch.
After the local sync succeeds, force-push the signed tip back to the source
remote, using a lease on the exact fetched commit ID.
The --remote argument is required; there is no default source remote.
Use --no-push to keep the result local.
Run from any worktree in the repository. If the branch is checked out in a
linked worktree, that worktree is updated. By default, dirty worktrees are
skipped with a warning, including staged, unstaged, untracked, and submodule
changes. Local commits absent from the freshly fetched source branch also cause
a warning and skip, including divergent history. Cached tracking refs are not
used to establish that local work has been pushed. Skips exit successfully
without replacing the branch. Use --allow-dirty and --allow-unpushed to override
these checks independently.
.PP
With --allow-dirty, local staged, unstaged, and untracked changes are stashed
immediately before replacing the branch,
then restored with their staging state after the sync. Ignored files are not
stashed. Worktrees with unresolved conflicts or an ongoing Git operation
are refused.
Other worktrees are untouched.
.PP
If restoring the stash fails, Git's conflict details, including affected paths,
are reported. The sync is reverted to the previous local tip
and the stash is reapplied there. A reverted sync exits with an error even
when the original changes were restored successfully. Existing stashes are
preserved. If recovery also fails, the saved stash is retained and its recovery
ref is printed. Fetching and signing happen before stashing, so failures in
either step leave the original worktree changes in place.
.PP
If BRANCH and --pr are omitted, use the current worktree's checked-out branch.
Detached HEAD requires an explicit BRANCH or --pr.
Supply either BRANCH or --pr, never both. The --pr option queries GitHub's API
through gh pr view to resolve the PR's head branch name. A number uses gh's
repository context unless --repo is supplied; a full PR URL selects the PR
directly. The resolved branch is fetched from the selected source remote.
.PP
If the branch has no local worktree, query the SSH remote's worktree list.
Mirror the branch's remote worktree path relative to the remote user's home
under the local home directory. For example, /home/alice/project-pr/topic
becomes ~/project-pr/topic. Both host:path and ssh://user@host:port/path URLs
are supported, using the ssh command and its normal SSH configuration.
.PP
Use --worktree PATH to override this location. An explicit path is required
for non-SSH transports, a branch without a remote worktree, a remote worktree
outside the remote home, or an unavailable SSH lookup. The destination must
not already exist. After successful signing, create the worktree and check out
the signed branch, creating the local branch if necessary. Signing failures
create no worktree. A dry run only reports the chosen path. Existing local
worktrees are reused; an explicit PATH must match an existing worktree.
.PP
Every run first fetches upstream master from origin, or main if master does
not exist on that remote. The advertised remote branches are checked even if
origin/HEAD is stale or the upstream tracking ref is missing locally.
Use --upstream REMOTE/BRANCH to select a different upstream.
.PP
Only commits absent from the fetched upstream history are signed. All commits
reachable from upstream keep their original IDs and signatures, including
commits brought in through merge parents. Local master/main branches are not
updated by the upstream fetch. Use --base REF to additionally exclude the
history of a stacked parent branch; it cannot disable upstream protection.
The explicit base ref is read locally. If the fetched branch is entirely
upstream history, it is synced without recreating or signing any commits.
An upstream fetch failure prevents signing and branch replacement.
.PP
Without --base, protect the most recent first-parent merge that brings in
commits outside the fetched upstream history, together with all its ancestors.
Only commits above this dependency merge are considered for signing. The merge
itself and imported dependencies retain their original IDs and signatures.
Merges of upstream alone do not establish this boundary; earlier topic commits
still need signing.
.PP
Also look for the nearest other-author commit below your own
commits on the first-parent history, using Git's current author email as your
identity. Check the branch tips currently advertised by configured remotes,
excluding the source remote. If a branch tip exactly matches that commit,
fetch it and protect the parent history from signing. Local tracking refs
may be missing or stale. If a dependency merge was detected, limit this search
to commits above it. Authorship alone never excludes commits. Neither detection
repairs parent commits that have already been rewritten.
.PP
Use --base REF to override both detections for unpublished parents, parents whose
remote branch has moved, or stacks using another author email. Passing the
upstream tracking ref explicitly signs the entire topic range. Unavailable
remotes are reported and skipped during detection; if no branch tip matches,
the signing range excludes upstream history and any detected dependency merge.
.PP
Existing signed topic commits are kept byte-for-byte, including
their IDs and signatures, when their parents are unchanged. Adding a new
unsigned commit to a signed branch signs only that new commit, retaining
fast-forward ancestry with the published branch. Repeated syncs do not
re-sign the existing history. Existing signatures are not verified, so
contributor public keys are not required. Only signatures created by this
utility are verified.
.PP
Unsigned topic commits, including merge and empty commits, are signed using
Git's configured signing key and program. If a parent is rewritten, descendants
must also be recreated and signed because their signed payload changes.
Commit trees, messages, author and
committer identities, dates, and parent ordering are preserved.
Topic parent IDs are replaced with their signed equivalents.
Unsupported extra commit headers (for example mergetag) cause an error when
a commit needs to be recreated.
.PP
Signing changes commit IDs. The local branch is replaced only after all new
commits pass signature and metadata verification. Signing failures leave the
branch and its checkout unchanged. Shallow repositories are rejected.
.PP
For SSH and local transports, the push invokes receive-pack with
receive.denyCurrentBranch=updateInstead for that invocation only.
Git updates a checked-out branch and its primary or linked worktree together,
without detaching HEAD or changing remote configuration. A dirty remote
index or worktree causes Git to refuse the push; remote edits are preserved.
The lease also rejects a remote branch changed since the fetch.
If pushing fails, restore the previous local tip, checkout, and original
staged, unstaged, and untracked changes by default, then exit with an error.
Remove worktrees and branches created by this invocation only if they remain
clean and unchanged. Concurrent local commits or conflicting edits prevent
destructive recovery; preserve them and report the retained recovery refs.
Fetched and signed commits remain reachable through recovery refs even after
successful rollback. A reverted local sync never pushes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-pr\fR NUMBER|URL
Resolve the branch name via GitHub. Mutually exclusive with BRANCH.
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR OWNER/REPO
Select the GitHub repository for a PR number. Requires --pr.
.TP
\fB\-\-worktree\fR PATH
Create the branch's missing worktree at PATH after signature verification.
By default, mirror its SSH remote worktree path under the local home.
This option overrides inference and is required if no path can be inferred.
Existing paths are refused; if the branch already has a local worktree,
its current path is accepted.
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR NAME
Required configured source remote. No default.
The signed branch is pushed back to this remote after the local sync.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow-dirty\fR
Stash and restore local edits instead of skipping a dirty target worktree.
This does not permit replacing unpushed commits; use --allow-unpushed as well
when both checks apply.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow-unpushed\fR
Allow replacement of local commits absent from the freshly fetched source
branch. The old tip remains in recovery refs. Dirty worktrees still require
--allow-dirty.
.TP
\fB\-\-no-push\fR
Keep the signed branch local without pushing it back to the source remote.
.TP
\fB\-\-upstream\fR REMOTE/BRANCH
Upstream branch to fetch and protect. Default: origin/master, falling back to
origin/main only when master does not exist on origin.
.TP
\fB\-\-base\fR REF
Additional local ref whose reachable commits are excluded from signing.
Overrides automatic detection of dependency merges and published parent branches.
Upstream history is always excluded; the explicit ref is read locally.
.TP
\fB\-\-dry-run\fR
Fetch and list the signing range without signing, replacing the local branch,
creating a worktree, stashing local changes, or pushing.
Upstream and stacked-parent tracking refs, fetched objects, and FETCH_HEAD may change;
the temporary source ref is removed.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Display usage.
.SH RECOVERY
Runs retain refs under refs/fetch-sign/TIMESTAMP-ID/: source is the fetched tip,
previous is the old local tip (if it existed), and signed is the resulting new
tip (once signing completes). A stash ref is also retained if stashed changes
could not be restored. The namespace is printed. These refs keep commits
reachable and can be used with git log, git diff, and git reset.
.PP
To restore a checked-out branch, run git reset --keep REF/previous from its
worktree, substituting the printed recovery namespace for REF. For a branch
that is not checked out, use git update-ref refs/heads/BRANCH REF/previous.
.PP
If automatic recovery failed, inspect the current worktree before retrying
git stash apply --index REF/stash. The retained stash also remains in the stash
list. Its recovery ref is removed only after a successful restoration.
.PP
Existing branch tracking configuration is preserved. The automatic push goes
to the selected source remote only.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
cd /path/to/repository
git fetch-sign --remote workstation
git fetch-sign --remote workstation --dry-run feature/example
git fetch-sign --remote workstation feature/example
git fetch-sign --remote workstation --pr 123 --repo owner/repo
git fetch-sign --remote workstation \\
  --pr https://github.com/owner/repo/pull/123 \\
  --worktree ../repository-pr/123-topic
.fi
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Python 3.10 or newer, Git, access to the source remote, and a working local Git
signing configuration (for PGP, user.signingkey and GnuPG with its private key).
The --pr option additionally requires gh with GitHub API access.
Automatic remote worktree path discovery requires SSH access and a POSIX shell.
.SH SEE ALSO
git-commit-tree(1), git-verify-commit(1), git-update-ref(1), git-stash(1)
